<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tongatapu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tongatapu is the main island of Tonga, in the southern part of the kingdom, and holds most of its people and the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nukualofa">Nukuʻalofa</a>. A low coral island around a shallow lagoon, it was the seat of the Tuʻi Tonga kings, whose royal tombs stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Lapaha</a> near Muʻa, and it has the trilithon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">George Tupou I</a> made Nukuʻalofa his capital in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongatapu.jpg" alt="Tongatapu" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Space Agency · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tonga_ESA363260.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He gained control of Vavaʻu in 1833, and when the Tuʻi Kanokupolu Aleamotuʻa died in November 1845 he succeeded to the title on 4 December as King George Tupou I, making Nukuʻalofa on <b>Tongatapu</b> his capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The early kings are remembered as living in eastern <b>Tongatapu</b>, at Heketā, where tradition credits the eleventh Tuʻi Tonga, Tuʻitātui, with raising the stone trilithon known as the Haʻamonga ʻa Maui around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pieces of the same pottery had been found long before, by Father Otto Meyer, a Catholic missionary, on Watom Island off New Britain in 1909, and by William C. McKern on <b>Tongatapu</b> in Tonga in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
